From 08c26ab7558ae090f61df3a4ddc2608a16c8bde7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 12:42:43 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 028/206] feat(shorebird): gen_snapshot link-data options, with
 validated input

Adds base_ct_link_data / patch_ct_link_data / base_ft_link_data /
base_dt_link_data to STRING_OPTIONS_LIST and wires them into
CreateAndWritePrecompiledSnapshot, ahead of both the kVMAOTAssembly early
return and Dart_Precompile(), in their _main order: CT (base+patch), FT, DT.

Verified against their binary, not just against the recon notes:
  - all four spellings produce byte-identical "Empty value for option <x>"
    messages to theirs (the string set the spellings were recovered from)
  - the pairing error is verbatim identical:
    "Error: --base_ct_link_data must be used with --patch_ct_link_data."
  - none of the four appear in --help --verbose, matching theirs (0 vs 0):
    they are bin options, not DEFINE_FLAG VM flags

Input validation, because these paths are now user-supplied and ReadStream's
bounds checks are ASSERTs that compile out in Release and Product:
  - zero-length files rejected (no valid encoding is zero bytes)
  - decoded counts and string lengths validated against the bytes actually
    remaining, before any reserve() or read loop
  - buffers over-allocated and padded with 0xFF

That padding byte is load-bearing and was wrong on the first attempt. The
decoder continues while a byte is <= 127 and stops at >= 128, so 0x00 is a
CONTINUATION byte: zero-padding invited the overrun it was meant to prevent.
Measured on a 4-byte corrupt file, the first ReadUnsigned ran 4831 bytes past
the end (PendingBytes() == -4831); with a 0xFF sentinel it stops 1 byte in
(-1), inside our own allocation. Only running it revealed this.

Readers gained an `ok` out-parameter. Without it a caller cannot distinguish a
correct parse of an empty table from a missing file, an unreadable file, or
corrupt data, since all of those return an empty object -- so a mistyped
filename would have produced an empty link table and still exited 0. All four
gen_snapshot options treat ok==false as fatal.

Observable assertion, both halves. Positive: a real file is written with the
real encoder, read back through the file-path reader, and the singleton is
shown to hold exactly its contents -- null before Initialize*, correct entry
count, num_cids and specific name/hash values after. Negative: a bogus path,
an empty file, a truncated file and an absurd count are each rejected rather
than silently yielding an empty table.

Six fixtures re-checked even though link_info.{h,cc} was untouched this round,
because gen_snapshot.cc is the binary that emits those artifacts:
ours-pre-D1 vs ours-now 36/36 identical, ours vs theirs 24/36 unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

